# 20. A Simple Mental Model

Remember this:

### `T`

I have the actual value.

```rust
let name: String = String::from("Daud");
```

### `&T`

I can read the value but don't own it.

```rust
let name_ref: &String = &name;
```

### `&mut T`

I can modify the value but don't own it.

```rust
let name_ref: &mut String = &mut name;
```

Think:

```text
String
   │
   ├── &String
   │     └── read-only borrow
   │
   └── &mut String
         └── mutable borrow
```

---

# 21. The Three Rules You Should Memorize

These are the big ones.

### Rule 1 — References don't take ownership

```rust
fn print_name(name: &String) {
    println!("{}", name);
}
```

The function borrows `name`.

---

### Rule 2 — Many readers are allowed

```rust
let a = &value;
let b = &value;
let c = &value;
```

Multiple immutable references are okay.

---

### Rule 3 — Only one mutable borrower at a time

```rust
let a = &mut value;
```

You can't simultaneously create another mutable reference or an overlapping immutable reference.

